From b29f74da89e14d39099cfd8e030fbc65ef95b3a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 20:30:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count what the arithmetic operators are handed, item 3-1's
 first measurement

Phase 3's ordering rests on the diagnosis that the boxes are minted by the OPERATORS,
whose operands arrive boxed from array elements and object fields. The half of that
nobody had counted is the one that decides whether any fast path can be fed: how often a
generic operator's two operands are already Numbers. This item paid for skipping that
count once already, with a bitwise emission that is correct on 15 semantics cases and
removes zero boxes on the corpus.

ArithmeticOperandDiagnostics counts it, one pair on each generic arithmetic and bitwise
operator (plus the JSNumber and JSString overrides, which a Number or String receiver
reaches instead of the base). Over the seven-suite driver:

  suite          generic invocations   both Numbers    share   boxes allocated
  Richards                    55 198         55 197  100.00%            13 659
  DeltaBlue                   14 533         14 532   99.99%             6 765
  RayTrace                   844 051        844 044  100.00%           841 017
  Box2D                   15 916 294     15 916 279  100.00%        11 434 706
  EarleyBoyer                 26 116         26 112   99.98%           563 997
  Crypto                  39 867 896     39 866 794  100.00%        42 410 739
  NavierStokes            17 094 558     17 094 557  100.00%        29 977 465
  TOTAL                   73 818 646     73 817 515  100.00%        85 248 348

Every generic arithmetic invocation on the corpus but 1 131 arrives with two Numbers, and
that population is 86.6% of every box the corpus allocates. So the guard a speculating
native form needs is a branch that predicts perfectly.

The number next to it is what re-specifies the item. The compiler's own proof --
isLeftNumber && isRightNumber, the gate every phase 3 item so far has widened -- reaches
556 053 of 73 818 646 invocations, 0.75%, and even that is generous: it counts the
AddValue(double) overload and `+` is the only operator that has one. Compile-time
provability reaches 0.75% of the arithmetic; run-time truth reaches 100.00% of it, and six
correct, invisible items sit in that gap.

Consequence, and it corrects this item's own re-specification: "storage plus an unboxed
element read" assumes the problem is where the value is kept. It is not -- the operator
already receives two Numbers whatever they are stored in. What it cannot do is hand one
back, because its consumer is a JSValue local, slot or element. The shared half is a
run-time-guarded specialization of an arithmetic expression TREE -- evaluate each leaf
once, test the leaves, compute in raw doubles, box only the root -- and the per-shape rows
already in the item say what that is worth: s = s + a[0] * 1.5 is 96 B and three boxes, of
which two are intermediates.

It also partly reverses item 3-8's "do not start as written" without contradicting it.
3-8 priced a run-time numeric guard at the LOCAL and found the whole local tier worth 0.36%
of the corpus's boxing; that stands, because it is about the local. The same speculation at
the OPERATOR reaches 86.6% of the boxes.

One smaller correction on the way: a numeric literal is ALREADY a native double, so the
second box in `a[0] * 1.5` is not VisitLiteral re-boxing it as an operand of that multiply
-- it is the compiler boxing a raw double it holds, because `*` has no JSValue x double
overload while `+` does.

The counter's first version read zero on all seven suites against 85 M boxes, because the
enable was inserted next to the first of two identical NumberBoxingDiagnostics.Reset()
pairs -- one in a call probe, one in the driver. ArithmeticOperandCensusTests is what
stops that recurring: five fixtures, three of which make the counter discriminate rather
than merely fire (a string operand, an un-coerced object, and proven-numeric locals that
must reach the generic path zero times).

Counters off by default; the emitter turns them on around the driver run only. No
behaviour change. Full repository suite 7 930 tests, 0 failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
